# 02-language.md §1: "`##` begins a documentation comment attached to the
# immediately following declaration." A doc comment therefore sits exactly
# where its declaration sits, and this case pins that in the token stream —
# the layer where it was wrong.
#
# The lexer used to treat a `##` line as layout-transparent, exactly like a
# plain `#` line: the DocComment token was emitted without running the
# line's own indentation through the indent stack. That put every doc
# comment on the wrong side of a block boundary, and both natural shapes
# were unparseable:
#
#   - as a block's FIRST line, DocComment preceded the INDENT that opens
#     the block, so the parser reported `expected an indented block`;
#   - at the OUTER level after a block, DocComment preceded the DEDENTs
#     that close it, so the enclosing suite's own member loop swallowed it
#     and reported `expected a member after doc comment/attribute`.
#
# Both are pinned below, and the token order is the whole point: read
# `INDENT` immediately before `DOCCOMMENT` at 29:5, and `DEDENT` immediately
# before `DOCCOMMENT` at 32:1. Reverse either and the corresponding shape
# stops parsing — which is what `check-doc-comment-positions` then proves
# at the parse/check layer.
#
# Found by `cargo xtask fuzz sema` (seed 9101), which mutated a doc comment
# into the position after a struct and caught the pretty-printer emitting
# source its own parser rejects.

struct Counter:
    ## The running total. First line of the block: INDENT, then DOCCOMMENT.
    n: u32

## Documented, and preceded by a block. DEDENT, then DOCCOMMENT.
struct Other:
    y: u32
